The happy integration test adopts terminal `t-17`, preserving its process PID
and start token, replays exit final offset 8, rejects acknowledgement at offset
7, then clears the retained record at offset 8. A typed Tail fixture round-trips
through the production encoder/decoder; this does not simulate a holder
retrieving tail bytes.

The failure integration test checks typed `unsupported_generation`,
`stale_target`, `truncated_frame`, `malformed_frame`, and `frame_too_large`
results. The adoption transition test covers fresh adopt, same-incarnation
re-adopt, stale incarnation, and wrong generation.

Property tests round-trip all 16 message variants at every two-part frame
boundary, exercise arbitrary fragment widths across all variants, and verify
identity u64 fields serialize as canonical decimal strings. The malformed
header decoder test also verifies the rejected partial frame is cleared.
Proptest uses an explicit source-relative regression file if it finds a
counterexample.

The model-based adoption property generates bounded sequences containing
Adopt, same/stale/wrong-generation Readopt, Exit(offset), and Ack(offset).
It checks `stale_target`, `unsupported_generation`, `exit_not_drained`, and
`exit_not_retained`; retained exits are compared to the reference model after
each transition and remain until acknowledgement reaches the final offset.

The large-counter JSON test sets all serialized u64 fields above 2^53 in each
message variant, uses `u32::MAX` for PID fields and `u16::MAX` for generation
fields, walks every emitted JSON value to reject unsafe numeric integers and
asserts the designated counter/PID/generation fields are decimal strings, then
verifies exact encode/decode equality.

